    {
      "id": "918acfbb-2634-4d30-9408-64cd09639e74",
      "name": "Workflow overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -2240,
        -640
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 496,
        "height": 848,
        "content": "## 09-05-07 stitch ui design bot with project memory\n\n### How it works\n- The chat panel and a Telegram bot both feed one normalized pipeline, so the same bot works on either channel.\n- Empty or tiny messages get a friendly hint right away and never reach the agent.\n- A Google Sheets store remembers each user; returning visitors get their past request injected back into the prompt so their Stitch project continues naturally.\n- A Gemini agent drives six Google Stitch MCP tools to create projects, list projects and screens, and generate UI from plain language, with web research available for follow-up facts.\n- Answers are converted to Telegram-safe HTML for the bot and kept as markdown in the chat panel.\n- Errors are caught, reported to an admin chat, and written to a log sheet.\n\n### Setup\n- Create a Google Sheets file with two tabs: ProjectMemory (headers session_id, channel, last_prompt, logged_at) and ErrorLog (headers session_id, error_message, logged_at).\n- Set the sheet URL in STITCH_PROJECT_MEMO_SHEET and the admin chat id in ADMIN_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID.\n- Add an HTTP Header Auth credential with the header X-Goog-Api-Key and your Stitch API key, then attach it to the six MCP tool nodes.\n- Add Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Telegram bot credentials to their nodes.\n- Activate the workflow and ask for a screen, for example: build a checkout page for a coffee shop.\n\n### Customization\n- Change the hint text, or remove the web research tool when search is not needed."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    }
  ],
